  "prediction": "**Oldest Cold Case Ever Brought to Court: Man Released After 58 Years**\n\nIn a historic turn of events, Jack McCullough, a 76-year-old man from Washington state, has been released from prison after serving a life sentence for the 1957 murder of 7-year-old Maria Ridulph. The case, which went unsolved for half a century, was one of the oldest cold cases in the United States.\n\nMaria Ridulph was abducted on December 3, 1957, while playing in the snow with a friend in Sycamore, Illinois. Her body was found five months later, and despite an extensive investigation by the FBI, no one was ever charged in connection with her murder.\n\nIn 2012, Jack McCullough was convicted of Maria's murder and sentenced to life in prison. However, a six-month review of evidence conducted by DeKalb County State's Attorney Richard Schmack raised questions about the conviction. Schmack concluded that McCullough could not have killed Maria, citing newly discovered phone records that supported McCullough's long-held alibi.\n\nThe records showed that McCullough made a collect call to his parents at 6:57 p.m. from a phone booth in downtown Rockford, which is 40 miles northwest of where Maria was abducted. Schmack also reviewed police reports and hundreds of other documents, including from the Air Force recruitment office, which he said had been improperly barred at trial.\n\nOn Friday, Judge William P. Brady ordered McCullough's release, citing the new evidence and the fact that it would have been impossible for McCullough to have been in two places at once. McCullough's family members were overjoyed by the decision, while Maria's family remains convinced that McCullough is guilty and has sought the appointment of a special prosecutor to keep him behind bars.\n\nThe case has raised questions about the reliability of eyewitness testimony and the importance of reviewing evidence in cold cases. It also highlights the challenges of prosecuting crimes that occurred decades ago, when evidence may be lost or destroyed and witnesses may have died.\n\nMcCullough's release is a significant development in the case, and it is likely to reopen old wounds in the community. However, it also brings a measure of justice to a case that has been shrouded in controversy for decades.",
